tate case. We are not mutating.\n\nEdge cases: If prediction_tokens is zero? The problem says \"Normalize gain by at least one prediction token.\" This suggests we divide by prediction_tokens, which should be positive. In fixtures, they are positive. If it's zero, we might raise ValueError as it's invalid. Our code does check tokens>0.\n\nNow produce the final JSON.\n\nWe'll also include docstring? Not necessary.\n\nImplementation function name is solve(case). Return dict.\n\nLet's write the Python code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    # Validate case structure\n    if not isinstance(case, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"case must be a dict\")\n    if 'threshold' not in case or 'calls' not in case:\n        raise ValueError(\"case missing threshold or calls\")\n    threshold = case['threshold']\n    calls = case['calls']\n    if not isinstance(calls, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"calls must be a list\")\n    accepted_call_ids = []\n    diagnostics = []\n    for call in calls:\n        if not isinstance(call, dict):\n            raise ValueError(\"each call must be a dict\")\n        required = ['call_id', 'loss_without_call', 'loss_with_call', 'prediction_tokens', 'parseable']\n        for field in required:\n            if field not in call:\n                raise ValueError(f\"call missing required field {field}\")\n        call_id = call['call_id']\n        loss_without = call['loss_without_call']\n        loss_with = call['loss_with_call']\n        tokens = call['prediction_tokens']\n        parseable = call['parseable']\n        # Type checks\n        if not isinstance(call_id, str):\n            raise ValueError(\"call_id must be string\")\n        if not isinstance(loss_without, (int, float)):\n            raise ValueError(\"loss_without_call must be a number\")\n        if not isinstance(loss_with, (int, float)):\n            raise ValueError(\"loss_with_call must be a number\")\n        if not isinstance(tokens, int) or tokens <= 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"prediction_tokens must be a positive integer\")\n        if not isinstance(parseable, bool):\n            raise ValueError(\"parseable must be a boolean\")\n        gain = loss_without - loss_with\n        normalized_gain = gain / tokens\n        accepted = parseable and normalized_gain >= threshold\n        if accepted:\n            accepted_call_ids.append(call_id)\n        diagnostics.append({\n            \"accepted\": accepted,\n            \"call_id\": call_id,\n            \"normalized_gain\": normalized_gain\n        })\n    return {\n        \"accepted_call_ids\": accepted_call_ids,\n        \"diagnostics\": diagnostics\n    }\n\nNow we need to embed this in a JSON string.\n\nWe'll write:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    # Validate case structure\\n    if not isinstance(case, dict):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"case must be a dict\\\")\\n    if 'threshold' not in case or 'calls' not in case:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"case missing threshold or calls\\\")\\n    threshold = case['threshold']\\n    calls = case['calls']\\n    if not isinstance(calls, list):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"calls must be a list\\\")\\n    accepted_call_ids = []\\n    diagnostics = []\\n    for call in calls:\\n        if not isinstance(call, dict):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"each call must be a dict\\\")\\n        required = ['call_id', 'loss_without_call', 'loss_with_call', 'prediction_tokens', 'parseable']\\n        for field in required:\\n            if field not in call:\\n                raise ValueError(f\\\"call missing required field {field}\\\")\\n        call_id = call['call_id']\\n        loss_without = call['loss_without_call']\\n        loss_with = call['loss_with_call']\\n        tokens = call['prediction_tokens']\\n        parseable = call['parseable']\\n        # Type checks\\n        if not isinstance(call_id, str):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"call_id must be string\\\")\\n        if not isinstance(loss_without, (int, float)):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"loss_without_call must be a number\\\")\\n        if not isinstance(loss_with, (int, float)):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"loss_with_call must be a number\\\")\\n        if not isinstance(tokens, int) or tokens <= 0:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"prediction_tokens must be a positive integer\\\")\\n        if not isinstance(parseable, bool):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"parseable must be a boolean\\\")\\n        gain = loss_without - loss_with\\n        normalized_gain = gain / tokens\\n        accepted = parseable and normalized_gain >= threshold\\n        if accepted:\\n            accepted_call_ids.append(call_id)\\n        diagnostics.append({\\n            \\\"accepted\\\": accepted,\\n            \\\"call_id\\\": call_id,\\n            \\\"normalized_gain\\\": normalized_gain\\n        })\\n    return {\\n        \\\"accepted_call_ids\\\": accepted_call_ids,\\n        \\\"diagnostics\\\": diagnostics\\n    }\"}\n\nWe need to escape double quotes inside the string: each double quote must be \\\". Also backslashes like \\n are already newline characters in JSON string? Actually in JSON string, we need to represent newline as \\n (two characters: backslash and n). But in Python source, we have literal newlines. In JSON, we must escape newlines as \\n. So we need to replace each newline in the Python code with \\n. Also each backslash (like in \\\\n if any) needs to be escaped as \\\\\\n? But we don't have backslashes in code except in f-strings? The f-string contains {field} but that's fine. The code doesn't contain backslashes. So we can just write the code with newlines and then escape them. Better to construct the JSON manually by taking the Python code string and replacing newlines with \\\\n. But careful: In JSON string, the backslash itself must be escaped, so to represent a newline character, we write \\n (backslash followed by n). So when we embed the Python code into a JSON string, we need to have \\n as two characters: backslash and n. In Python string, if we write a literal newline, it's fine, but in JSON it must be escaped. So we can write the entire JSON object with the implementation string containing escaped newlines.\n\nWe'll produce the JSON string directly. It's easier to generate programmatically, but we are writing by hand. We'll double-check.\n\nI'll write the answer.\n\nAlso ensure there is no trailing whitespace.\n\nThe response must be exactly the JSON object, no surrounding quotes or code fences.\n\nThus final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2171,"completion_tokens":3606,"total_tokens":5777,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3063},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":123},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}